+\subsection{AnimatLab Conversion Variants}
+
+Table~\ref{tab:app_spk_animatlab_full} catalogues every spiking copy built from the AnimatLab standalone exports. The conversion rules, applied by \texttt{tools/make\_spiking.py} to copies (no original file touched), are: thresholds of neurons with unreachable (positive) thresholds move into the spiking regime --- \qty{-55}{\mV}, \qty{-59}{\mV} for motor-neuron-named cells in the v1 copies, and per-class calibrated values from the measured graded peaks in the v2 copies (rhythm-generator/pattern-formation/interneuron \qty{-53.5}{\mV}, motor neurons \qty{-44}{\mV}, Renshaw \qty{-44.5}{\mV}); relative accommodation is zeroed; every graded chemical synapse type is re-emitted as a per-spike type with the same identifier and amplitude, \qty{10}{\ms} decay, and maximum conductance $\max(5,\,2\times$amplitude$)$; and each connection's own class tag is flipped to the spiking enum. That last rule is a documented format trap: the per-connection \texttt{<Type>} tag is stored independently of the synapse type, and converting only the type kills the run at load with no diagnostic detail. Structural validation confirms for every copy that neuron, connection, synapse-type, chart, and stimulus sets are identical to the original and that every class tag matches its synapse type.
